.TH CHOWN 2 "2026-10-19" "Substrate" "System Calls"
.SH NAME
chown, fchown, lchown \- change the owner and group of a file
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library (libc, \-lc)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <unistd.h>
.PP
.BI "int chown(const char *" pathname ", uid_t " owner ", gid_t " group );
.BI "int fchown(int " fd ", uid_t " owner ", gid_t " group );
.BI "int lchown(const char *" pathname ", uid_t " owner ", gid_t " group );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
These calls change the owner and group of a file:
.BR chown ()
the file
.I pathname
names, following symbolic links;
.BR fchown ()
the open file
.IR fd ;
.BR lchown ()
like
.BR chown ()
but a symbolic link itself, not its target.
An
.I owner
or
.I group
of \-1 leaves that ID as it is.
.PP
The superuser may set any owner and group.
The file's owner may set the owner only to itself, and the group only to
its effective group ID or one of its supplementary groups.
No other process may change either.
.PP
When anyone but the superuser changes the owner or group, the
set-user-ID and set-group-ID bits of the file's mode are cleared.
The file's change time is updated, and the change is written to the file
system.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, zero is returned.
On error, \-1 is returned, and
.I errno
is set to indicate the error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EBADF
.RB ( fchown ())
.I fd
is not an open file descriptor.
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I pathname
points outside the accessible address space.
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I owner
or
.I group
is negative and not \-1.
.TP
.B ENOENT
The file does not exist.
.TP
.B EPERM
The caller is not the superuser, and either does not own the file, asked
for a different owner, or asked for a group it is not a member of.
.TP
.B EROFS
The file is on a read-only file system.
.SH EXAMPLE
Give a file to the group
.B staff
(gid 50), of which the calling user is a member, keeping its owner:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
if (chown("report.txt", (uid_t)\-1, 50) == \-1)
    perror("chown");
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR chown (1),
.BR chmod (2),
.BR fchownat (2),
.BR setgroups (2),
.BR stat (2)
